    "issued_at": "2026-10-21T08:01:00+00:00",
    "product_id": "42efba26-4503-40df-8ed8-cb00fa6d7e2c",
    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 210801\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n101 AM PDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Multiple rounds of rain and storms expected northwest of \n  Interstate 15 and in northwestern Arizona through Thursday.\n\n* The greatest risk of flash flooding and severe storms exists over \n  northwestern Arizona.\n  \n* Dry conditions and seasonable temperatures expected this weekend \n  and early next week. \n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...through Tuesday.\n\nLatest satellite imagery shows our system spinning off the CA coast \nin the form of a closed low. As expected, scattered showers and \nstorms have developed across our northern/northwestern zones \novernight in the left exit region of an upper-level jet streak. This \nactivity is forecast to continue through the day across Inyo, Nye, \nand Esmeralda counties, though coverage and intensity will vary over \ntime. A secondary area of convection is beginning to form across \nMohave, Lincoln, and northeastern Clark counties this morning as the \njet streak translates east and robust moisture advection occurs. \nPWATs between 1-1.25\" should allow for fairly efficient rainfall \nwithin this convective regime, though quick storm motions mean any \nflash flooding will likely require training convection. Best chances \nof this are in northern Mohave County, where a Flash Flood Watch is \nin effect through Thursday afternoon. In addition to the flood \nthreat, northwestern AZ will have the risk of severe thunderstorms \ntoday, and to a lesser extent on Thursday. The SPC has given this \narea a marginal to slight risk of severe storms, including the \npotential for a tornado or two, damaging winds, and hail up to 2\" in \ndiameter today. Anomalous low-level moisture and decent lapse rates \nwill foster CAPE values between 500-1500 J/kg. Hodograph curvature \nis limited, but low-level wind shear is rather impressive for the \narea (25-35 knots in the lowest 3km based on forecast soundings). \nThese long, straight hodographs will favor splitting supercells, \nwith the left split carrying the greatest hail risk and the right \nsplit having the greatest tornado risk. However, abundant storm \ncoverage/interactions may complicate or limit the severe threat. The \nsevere risk may carry over into the overnight hours tonight if \nstorms manage to stay in our portion of AZ.\n\nThe primary vort max of the low/trough swings through on Thursday, \nbringing the best chance of widespread precipitation to our region. \nPoPs on Thursday are 50% or more in most locations, though the \nsouthern Mojave Desert remains the greatest area of \"bust\" potential \nwith PoPs 10-40%. Precipitation chances largely exit the CWA \nThursday night once the shortwave trough axis clears us, but a \nsecondary shortwave diving south will keep the larger trough \noverhead on Friday. This allows shower chances (20-40%) to linger, \nprimarily in the southern Great Basin. The trough finally pushes \neast on Saturday, although guidance has trended slower with this. \nThe weekend should be much drier, but if the trough continues to \nslow, low precipitation chances may linger in our eastern areas on \nSaturday. \n\nThe hydro/convective concerns are going to be the primary focus with \nthis system, but there are other aspects worth mentioning as well. \nSnow levels bottom out around 8kft, but most of the precip will fall \nwhile they are 9kft+, thus limiting any winter impacts to the higher \nelevations of the Sierra. Gusty winds will be present as well with \nthis storm, with widespread southerly gusts of 20-40 mph today and \nThursday. Friday into Saturday, we see a shift to gusty north winds \nas the system pulls away. Temperatures will be near normal today, \ndropping to below-normal values tomorrow through Saturday. Dry and \nseasonable conditions expected Sunday through Tuesday as we see a \nlull in the storm track.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 06Z Forecast \nPackage...Southwesterly winds persist overnight before favoring \nthe east tomorrow morning, with speeds of 8 knots or less. \nElevated southerly to southeasterly winds arrive by the afternoon,\nwith peak gusts between 25 and 30 knots. Winds weaken after \nsunset and shift to the southwest overnight. Bases may lower to \naround 8kft on Wednesday night. \n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 06Z Forecast Package...Southerly to \nsoutheasterly winds pick up across much of the area tomorrow \nafternoon, with peak gusts between 25 and 30 knots. These winds \nshould weaken in the afternoon. Precipitation will stay out of the \nvicinity of most terminals, except for KBIH, where thunderstorm \nactivity will pick up in the late morning and afternoon. The highest \nconfidence for rainfall at the terminal is between 20Z and 24Z. \nCloud bases lower to as low as 7kft tomorrow night in Owens Valley \nbehind the thunderstorms. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Woods\nAVIATION...Meltzer\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
